parse_wkb_posns: fix uint32 overflow in the point-count bounds check

The fast path for 2-D native-endian WKB validates the point count with
`len-i >= count*2*8`. The right-hand side is evaluated in uint32_t, so any
count >= 0x10000000 wraps (e.g. count = 0x20000000 gives count*16 == 0).
A crafted WKB blob with such a count passes the bounds check and
process_points then reads far past the end of the input buffer.

Compare in the size_t domain instead, and do the index advance with the
count widened to size_t.

Found by fuzzing WKB inputs through sqlite-tg; reachable from any WKB
parse entry point (tg_parse_wkb and friends).

diff --git a/tg.c b/tg.c
--- a/tg.c
+++ b/tg.c
@@ -12272,11 +12272,15 @@ static size_t parse_wkb_posns(enum base base, int dims,
     uint32_t count;
     read_uint32(count);
     if (count == 0) return i;
-    if (dims == 2 && !swap && len-i >= count*2*8) {
-        // Use the point data directly. No allocations. 
+    // PATCH(sqlite-tg): compare in the size_t domain. The upstream check
+    // `len-i >= count*2*8` evaluates the right side in uint32_t, which wraps
+    // for count >= 0x10000000, letting a crafted WKB point count pass the
+    // bounds check and drive an out-of-bounds read in process_points.
+    if (dims == 2 && !swap && (size_t)count <= (len-i)/16) {
+        // Use the point data directly. No allocations.
         *points = (void*)(wkb+i);
         *npoints = count;
-        i += count*2*8; 
+        i += (size_t)count*16;
     } else {
         for (uint32_t j = 0 ; j < count; j++) {
             read_posn(posn);
